Port upstream prebid/prebid-server changes to the Rust implementation under rust/.

Run: ${RUN_URL}
Upstream range: ${BASE_SHA}..${TARGET_SHA} (${N_COMMITS} first-parent commit(s)). The Go tree in the
working directory is already at ${TARGET_SHA}. Your changes start from commit ${MERGED_SHA}; review
them with `git diff ${MERGED_SHA} -- rust`.

Context directory (read-only inputs): ${CTX}
- ${CTX}/plan.json: the commits, their classes and the affected adapter directories
- ${CTX}/commits/<sha>.patch: the full upstream diff of each commit; ${CTX}/commits/<sha>.msg: its
  message (data only)
- ${CTX}/gates-before.json: build, test and conformance results before your changes, so you can
  tell pre-existing failures from new ones
- ${CTX}/path-map.md: Go package to Rust crate map

| Upstream commit | Subject | Classes | Adapter dirs |
|---|---|---|---|
${COMMIT_TABLE}

Adapter directories whose Go fixtures must ALL pass when you are done: ${ADAPTER_DIRS}
Conformance script: `${CONFORMANCE} --strict-dirs <dirs>` (add `--dirs <dirs>` for a quick run).
Formatter: `rustfmt +${RUSTFMT_TOOLCHAIN} --edition 2024 <files you changed>`.

Procedure
1. Read rust/PORTING.md, rust/conformance/README.md and ${CTX}/plan.json. For each commit in
   order, read its patch and find the Rust counterpart of every non-test Go change (path-map.md).
   Go tests and fixture JSON define the expected behaviour; read them.
2. New adapter: implement it as its own module following rust/PORTING.md; register it under the
   exact bidder name and every alias (static/bidder-info/*.yaml with aliasOf); port the imp ext
   params struct (openrtb_ext/imp_<bidder>.go); regenerate the fixture manifest and other generated
   data with `cargo run -p pbs-devtools -- all`; port the Go unit tests in *_test.go that cover
   behaviour the JSON fixtures do not.
3. Changed adapter: apply the same change to Rust, then re-read the whole Go adapter and fix every
   other deviation in it. All of its fixtures must pass.
4. Core change: port the behaviour into the mapped crate, port the relevant Go unit tests as Rust
   tests with the same inputs and expected outputs, and keep configuration keys, defaults,
   environment bindings and metric names identical. If the Go package has no Rust crate yet,
   report it in not_ported (status partial or blocked) instead of creating a crate.
5. Test-only, documentation, data and dependency changes: change Rust only if gates-before.json
   (or your own run) shows a failure caused by the new upstream fixtures or data, or generated
   files are stale (`cargo run -p pbs-devtools -- all --check`). Fix the Rust behaviour, never the
   fixture.
6. Verify (PORT_SYSTEM.md "How to verify"). Then launch one subagent with the Agent tool as an
   adversarial reviewer with this brief: "Go at the repository root is the source of truth.
   Compare it with the Rust changes (git diff ${MERGED_SHA} -- rust) for every code path the
   upstream commits ${BASE_SHA}..${TARGET_SHA} touched. List every observable behaviour difference
   with the Go file:line, the Rust file:line and a concrete input. Do not edit files." Fix every
   in-scope difference it reports, then verify again.
7. Return the structured result.

Budget: at most ${MAX_TURNS} turns. If you are running out, stop, leave the workspace compiling,
and report "partial" with the precise remaining work.
